1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:07,095
What is a Git repository?

2
00:00:07,633 --> 00:00:10,501
What makes a folder a Git repository:

3
00:00:10,501 --> 00:00:13,988
the hidden .git folder, and what it contains.

4
00:00:15,733 --> 00:00:20,133
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

5
00:00:20,667 --> 00:00:22,586
Inside .git:

6
00:00:23,133 --> 00:00:24,256
Inside .git:

7
00:00:24,256 --> 00:00:28,558
The most important small files, read directly:

8
00:00:29,100 --> 00:00:32,690
The most important small files, read directly:

9
00:00:32,690 --> 00:00:40,808
HEAD says "the current branch is main"; refs/heads/main holds a commit ID; that ID is the latest commit.

10
00:00:40,808 --> 00:00:46,350
A branch is just a file with a commit ID in it (lesson 77 goes deeper).

11
00:00:48,100 --> 00:00:49,912
Let me try something.

12
00:00:49,912 --> 00:00:53,450
Run a Git command outside any repository:

13
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:54,427
fatal:

14
00:00:54,427 --> 00:00:58,417
not a git repository (or any of the parent directories):

15
00:00:58,417 --> 00:00:58,773
.git:

16
00:00:58,773 --> 00:01:03,617
Git looked for .git here and in every parent folder, and found none.

17
00:01:03,617 --> 00:01:10,670
Either you are in the wrong folder, or the project was never initialised (or someone deleted .git).

18
00:01:10,670 --> 00:01:12,094
Check where you are:

19
00:01:13,833 --> 00:01:17,768
Go to the project folder (here, the lab):

20
00:01:19,533 --> 00:01:20,247
Got it.

21
00:01:20,247 --> 00:01:22,998
What do I need to remember?

22
00:01:23,533 --> 00:01:29,188
A repository is the .git folder; the files around it are the working directory.

23
00:01:29,733 --> 00:01:34,738
HEAD names the current branch; a branch is a file holding a commit ID.

24
00:01:35,300 --> 00:01:35,779
fatal:

25
00:01:35,779 --> 00:01:39,691
not a git repository means no .git here or above:

26
00:01:39,691 --> 00:01:42,484
wrong folder, or never initialised.

27
00:01:44,233 --> 00:01:46,991
I keep hearing about git init.

28
00:01:46,991 --> 00:01:48,738
What is it, really?

29
00:01:49,300 --> 00:01:56,154
Turning a folder into a repository with git init, and exactly what that does (and does not do).

30
00:01:57,900 --> 00:02:02,299
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

31
00:02:02,833 --> 00:02:04,433
Let's run it.

32
00:02:04,967 --> 00:02:09,898
What happened, exactly:

33
00:02:09,898 --> 00:02:14,829
a .git folder appeared.

34
00:02:14,829 --> 00:02:17,616
Nothing else.

35
00:02:18,167 --> 00:02:24,564
What happened, exactly:

36
00:02:24,564 --> 00:02:30,961
a .git folder appeared.

37
00:02:30,961 --> 00:02:34,576
Nothing else.

38
00:02:36,333 --> 00:02:37,940
Let me try something.

39
00:02:37,940 --> 00:02:39,777
The most common mistake:

40
00:02:39,777 --> 00:02:44,063
git init in the wrong place, typically your home folder.

41
00:02:44,600 --> 00:02:48,590
git status inside website shows files from the wrong level,

42
00:02:48,590 --> 00:02:53,661
and --show-toplevel reveals the repository root is home-sim, two levels up:

43
00:02:53,661 --> 00:02:58,057
every folder below it is now "inside" that accidental repository.

44
00:02:58,057 --> 00:02:59,544
Symptoms in real life:

45
00:02:59,544 --> 00:03:06,644
git status listing your whole home folder, or a project that suddenly "has" thousands of untracked files.

46
00:03:08,400 --> 00:03:12,893
Remove the accidental .git (only the one you created by mistake:

47
00:03:12,893 --> 00:03:17,105
check the path first!), then initialise in the right folder:

48
00:03:18,867 --> 00:03:19,580
Got it.

49
00:03:19,580 --> 00:03:22,331
What do I need to remember?

50
00:03:22,867 --> 00:03:28,351
git init creates .git; existing files stay untracked until you add them.

51
00:03:28,900 --> 00:03:35,389
A new branch has no commit, so refs/heads/main does not exist until the first commit.

52
00:03:35,933 --> 00:03:42,603
Check where you are before git init; git rev-parse --show-toplevel shows which repository you are in.

53
00:03:44,367 --> 00:03:47,056
How does the working directory work?

54
00:03:47,600 --> 00:03:50,840
The working directory is where you edit files.

55
00:03:50,840 --> 00:03:56,756
Git compares it with the last commit and tells you what is new, modified or deleted.

56
00:03:56,756 --> 00:04:01,475
We create, change and delete files and watch how Git sees each one.

57
00:04:03,233 --> 00:04:07,633
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

58
00:04:08,167 --> 00:04:09,682
Clean to start with:

59
00:04:09,682 --> 00:04:13,166
the working directory matches the last commit.

60
00:04:13,166 --> 00:04:15,741
Now three kinds of change at once:

61
00:04:16,300 --> 00:04:18,946
Now three kinds of change at once:

62
00:04:18,946 --> 00:04:20,113
Git sorts them:

63
00:04:20,113 --> 00:04:20,813
modified:

64
00:04:20,813 --> 00:04:22,448
menu.txt and deleted:

65
00:04:22,448 --> 00:04:28,673
prices.txt under Changes not staged for commit, hours.txt under Untracked files.

66
00:04:28,673 --> 00:04:30,541
Nothing is recorded yet:

67
00:04:30,541 --> 00:04:33,264
the commit history has not changed.

68
00:04:33,800 --> 00:04:34,994
Git sorts them:

69
00:04:34,994 --> 00:04:35,710
modified:

70
00:04:35,710 --> 00:04:37,382
menu.txt and deleted:

71
00:04:37,382 --> 00:04:43,749
prices.txt under Changes not staged for commit, hours.txt under Untracked files.

72
00:04:43,749 --> 00:04:45,659
Nothing is recorded yet:

73
00:04:45,659 --> 00:04:48,445
the commit history has not changed.

74
00:04:50,200 --> 00:04:51,585
Let me try something.

75
00:04:51,585 --> 00:04:55,739
You worked for an hour and want everything back the way it was,

76
00:04:55,739 --> 00:05:00,619
so you delete the folder's files by hand and copy old ones from somewhere.

77
00:05:01,167 --> 00:05:06,304
pathspec 'menu.text' did not match any file(s) known to git:

78
00:05:06,304 --> 00:05:09,901
the path is wrong (.text instead of .txt).

79
00:05:09,901 --> 00:05:12,984
Git can only restore paths it knows.

80
00:05:12,984 --> 00:05:14,696
List what it tracks:

81
00:05:16,433 --> 00:05:21,547
menu.txt and prices.txt are back to their committed content.

82
00:05:21,547 --> 00:05:27,087
And after git add -A, the rename from the exercise is recognised:

83
00:05:27,087 --> 00:05:29,048
R README.md -> INFO.md.

84
00:05:30,800 --> 00:05:31,513
Got it.

85
00:05:31,513 --> 00:05:34,264
What do I need to remember?

86
00:05:34,800 --> 00:05:40,904
The working directory is your files as they are right now; Git compares them with the last commit.

87
00:05:41,467 --> 00:05:44,462
Three states for a changed file:

88
00:05:44,462 --> 00:05:47,176
modified, deleted, untracked.

89
00:05:47,733 --> 00:05:53,648
Nothing is recorded until you commit; git restore brings back the committed version of a file.

90
00:05:55,400 --> 00:05:57,719
Why would I need git status?

91
00:05:58,267 --> 00:06:00,834
How to read every part of git status:

92
00:06:00,834 --> 00:06:05,552
the branch line, the remote line, and the three sections of changes.

93
00:06:05,552 --> 00:06:09,091
It is the command you will run more than any other.

94
00:06:10,833 --> 00:06:15,233
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

95
00:06:15,767 --> 00:06:19,160
A clone of a remote repository, untouched:

96
00:06:19,160 --> 00:06:23,281
Now one change of each kind, plus one local commit:

97
00:06:23,833 --> 00:06:28,209
Now one change of each kind, plus one local commit:

98
00:06:28,209 --> 00:06:30,097
Read it top to bottom:

99
00:06:30,097 --> 00:06:30,268
1.

100
00:06:30,268 --> 00:06:31,555
On branch main:

101
00:06:31,555 --> 00:06:33,186
the current branch.

102
00:06:33,186 --> 00:06:33,357
2.

103
00:06:33,357 --> 00:06:36,360
ahead of 'origin/main' by 1 commit:

104
00:06:36,360 --> 00:06:42,366
one commit exists here and not yet on the remote (push it, lesson 42).

105
00:06:42,366 --> 00:06:42,538
3.

106
00:06:43,100 --> 00:06:45,072
Read it top to bottom:

107
00:06:45,072 --> 00:06:45,251
1.

108
00:06:45,251 --> 00:06:46,596
On branch main:

109
00:06:46,596 --> 00:06:48,299
the current branch.

110
00:06:48,299 --> 00:06:48,478
2.

111
00:06:48,478 --> 00:06:51,616
ahead of 'origin/main' by 1 commit:

112
00:06:51,616 --> 00:06:57,890
one commit exists here and not yet on the remote (push it, lesson 42).

113
00:06:57,890 --> 00:06:58,069
3.

114
00:06:59,833 --> 00:07:01,376
Let me try something.

115
00:07:01,376 --> 00:07:04,169
Read git status wrongly and lose work:

116
00:07:04,169 --> 00:07:11,443
you think prices.txt is staged, commit, and push, but the latte price change was not in the commit.

117
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:18,540
After the commit, git status still lists M prices.txt (and the extra matcha line in menu.txt):

118
00:07:18,540 --> 00:07:22,575
those changes were not staged, so they were not committed.

119
00:07:22,575 --> 00:07:26,820
The commit contains only what was in Changes to be committed:

120
00:07:28,567 --> 00:07:31,583
Stage the missing change and commit it (or,

121
00:07:31,583 --> 00:07:38,246
if the commit is not pushed yet, add it to the last commit with git commit --amend, lesson 11):

122
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:40,713
Got it.

123
00:07:40,713 --> 00:07:43,464
What do I need to remember?

124
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:45,175
git status:

125
00:07:45,175 --> 00:07:51,479
branch, ahead/behind, then staged / not staged / untracked.

126
00:07:52,033 --> 00:07:55,773
Only Changes to be committed goes into the next commit.

127
00:07:56,333 --> 00:07:57,714
git status -sb:

128
00:07:57,714 --> 00:08:03,883
two columns (staging area, working directory) plus the branch line.
